[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: The inter‑router link (router1:eth0) and the eBGP peering from router1 to router2.  
- Mechanism: With router1’s eth0 down, the eBGP session to router2 cannot establish, so router1 learns no route to the partner LAN 200.1.1.0/24. When pc1 (195.11.14.2) sends traffic to pc2 (200.1.1.2), router1 lacks a route and immediately returns ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable,” causing complete loss of connectivity.  
- Localization evidence: On router1, eth0 is DOWN (ip addr shows eth0 state DOWN; ifconfig flags lack UP/RUNNING). Router1’s BGP summary shows the neighbor 193.10.11.2 in Active state (not Established). From router2, pings to 193.10.11.1 fail with “Destination Host Unreachable,” confirming the interconnect is not reachable. pc1→pc2 pings receive “From 195.11.14.1 Destination Net Unreachable,” and router1’s routing table contains only its directly connected 195.11.14.0/24 with no 200.1.1.0/24 learned.  
- Root cause: Router1’s interconnect interface eth0 is down, breaking the eBGP session and removing reachability to 200.1.1.0/24.  
- Corrective fix: Do not restart router2. Restore the inter‑router link by bringing up router1’s eth0 (e.g., ip link set dev eth0 up) or restarting router1’s networking if needed. After eth0 comes up, the BGP session to 193.10.11.2 should establish and routes to 200.1.1.0/24 will be installed, restoring pc1↔pc2 connectivity. If a device must be restarted, restart router1 (not router2). 
=== END ===